# Clean Code

Source: Robert C. Martin, _Clean Code_. Inventory: `docs/clean-code-inventory.md`.

## Meaningful Names (Ch.2)

Prefer intention-revealing, searchable, pronounceable names. One word per concept. No Hungarian / type prefixes. Classes are nouns; methods are verbs. Prefer problem-domain vocabulary in feature/domain code; solution-domain (framework) words where they fit.

### Domain vocabulary

- Resume content: `resume`, `intro`, `summary`, `contact`, `experience`, `projects`, `skillGroups`, `education`, `additional`
- Evidence / IA: `evidenceIds`, `visibleBullets`, `expandedBullets`, section anchors
- Presentation: hero, lede, timeline, spotlight, sticky nav, badge groups
- Prefer content field names that match `src/content/resume.ts` over vague `data` / `items` / `info`

## Functions (Ch.3)

Small; do one thing; one abstraction level; stepdown (caller above callee). Prefer 0–2 args; at most 3 positional — more → named argument object type. Avoid flag arguments. No hidden side effects. Command/query separation. Prefer extract to small helpers or section components over growing past size gates (strict on `.ts`; TSX sections may be slightly larger — see inventory exceptions).

## Comments (Ch.4)

Prefer rename/extract over narrative comments. Keep legal, intent, clarification, and warning comments. Ban `FIXME` / `XXX` / `HACK`; temporary `TODO` OK with why. Delete commented-out code.

## Formatting (Ch.5)

Team formatter (Prettier, when installed) is horizontal source of truth. Vertical: newspaper metaphor, single blank between concepts, density within a thought, dependents below callers, conceptual affinity.

## Objects and Data Structures (Ch.6)

Objects hide data behind operations; data structures expose data. Treat `resume` content as a data structure; UI components render it. Avoid hybrids and Active Record on content payloads. Law of Demeter: talk to neighbors — reading resume fields is fine.

## Error Handling (Ch.7)

Prefer exceptions (or idiomatic Result-style outcomes) over error codes for unexpected failures. No empty catches. Provide context. Don’t use throw for expected validation — return a named outcome. Be deliberate with null/optional (e.g. open-ended `end` dates).

## Boundaries (Ch.8)

Wrap third-party APIs behind owned façades when any appear. Features depend on what you control. Static content and `next/font` stay app-owned; do not invent a backend for V1.

## Classes (Ch.10)

Small, cohesive, single responsibility. Prefer functions and typed modules for this resume app unless a class clearly earns its keep.

## Systems (Ch.11)

Separate construction from use. One-way module dependencies: content → UI, not the reverse. Keep the app static and testable.

## Emergence (Ch.12)

Runs all the tests; no duplication; expresses intent; fewest elements that still pass tests and keep SRP.

## Concurrency (Ch.13)

Prefer clear async boundaries. Snapshot when loads can go stale. OS thread material is N/A for this static Next.js site.

## Successive Refinement (Ch.14)

Make it work, then refine with the bar green. Feature branch; no broken main. Commit when the human asks.

## Smells and Heuristics (Ch.17)

Prefer MIT clone/dead-code tooling (unicorn, knip, jscpd). Avoid nested ternaries and lonely ifs when a clearer structure exists. Keep cyclomatic gates under Functions (Ch.3).
